{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1548175-redefining-the-penitent-legion","id":1548175,"title":"Redefining the Penitent Legion","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-02-04T00:02:55-08:00","id":1548175,"title":"Redefining the Penitent Legion","content":"I'll start by admitting I don't have a lot of knowledge when it comes to Wraith lore, but I've been diving into it and it's fascinating.<br /> <br /> That said, something I have not been very keen on is the way Wraith defines the Penitent Legion as composed of those who died of &quot;madness&quot;. I've always felt it a bit awkward at best and potentially problematic. It seems to me like it was one of those concepts at the time present in all splats. Like if White Wolf wanted to have Malkavians in every single game line. Let's say the idea of psychosis present in those days feels a bit outdated tbh.<br /> <br /> Anyway, I was wondering if I were to redefine the Legion, would it be viable or would it imply drastically changing other aspects of the setting. So here's my idea:<br /> <br /> What if the Penitent Legion were comprised of former criminals or suspected conspirators with Oblivion, former Renegades and Heretics and other alleged enemies of the Hierarchy who then are claimed by the Laughing Lady instead of going straight to the forge. A type of &quot;suicide squad&quot;- type of Legion if you will. I'm also thinking it could include something like the Orphan-Grinders from Orpheus, that is wraiths who somehow where able to come back from what seemed like imminent Spectrerhood. (Although as far I know that's not possible in WTO, right?)<br /> <br /> I think it would still work because they can still be distrusted by other the legions and they can be marked by blood as a mark of shame the other denizens of Stygia might be able to recognize. Other wraiths might consider them to be &quot;specters in training&quot; and avoid them.<br /> <br /> The Seat of Succor can still work like a prison/torture chamber where the Laughing Lady revels in psychological an corporal torment as means of interrogation for good of Stygia, of course...<br /> <br /> I have no idea if this overlaps with the purview of other Deadlords or anything else. It was just a passing but I wanted to know what you guys think. How would you improve the Penitent Legion? Or do you think it's perfect the way it is?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12201-brainstr\u00f6m","name":"Brainstr\u00f6m","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-05T06:41:26-08:00","id":1548220,"title":"","content":"If the legion was comprised of people who died as criminals (execution or died in prison or died in exile) that might work and it would fit a somewhat similar theme. It also works if that includes people who were traitors in life. Other legions would likely have a claim on most potential members, but that's the same situation currently.<br /> <br /> Traitors in death, though, is a harder sell. The forges of Stygia are always hungry so it's difficult to see how there could be a legion of Wraiths that would normally be soulforged. I could see a large Renegade faction that worked this way, but not a Legion.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-02-05T10:34:52-08:00","id":1548228,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brainstr\u00f6m</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1548175-redefining-the-penitent-legion#post1548175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, something I have not been very keen on is the way Wraith defines the Penitent Legion as composed of those who died of &quot;madness&quot;. I've always felt it a bit awkward at best and potentially problematic. It seems to me like it was one of those concepts at the time present in all splats. Like if White Wolf wanted to have Malkavians in every single game line. Let's say the idea of psychosis present in those days feels a bit outdated tbh.</div> </div> </div> </div>The Legions as a whole should represent the entirety of how people can die so that everyone can be slotted into the appropriate Legion. Of course, while most people fall into easy categories, there are always those whose deaths don't fit into easy categories. That's the distinction between the &quot;big&quot; Legions (Grim, Skeletal, Iron) and the others. (Although I've long thought that the Silent Legion should probably be one of the major ones because while they take in suicides, I think they also take in famine victims who also die in &quot;despair&quot;, and people dying from starvation or similar probably far outnumber those of suicides).<br /> <br /> So I view the Penitent Legion as being about &quot;senseless&quot; deaths. Deaths that don't make sense to the living and the newly departed. Anytime someone would consider their death shocking or senseless, it is a good candidate for the Penitent Legion. So it's not necessarily about madness in a clinical or psychological sense. It's about the lack of meaning, fairness, or expectation and what it implies about the world.<br /> <br /> There are plenty of people who can be expected to die from violence for example. Soldiers, police, criminals, civilians in warzones or high crime areas, and so on can be expected to possibly be killed. But when someone is randomly murdered in different scenario then such a death could be considered to be senseless. That might be because some crazy people randomly kills someone in a good part of town. But it might also be because some idiot general ordered an attack that served no purpose whatsoever and was doomed to fail. That isn't just a blunder or failure, but something truly meaningless.<br /> <br /> Likewise people die in car accidents all the time, and those are mostly covered by the Emerald Legion. But some car accident deaths might seem particularly non-sensical or out of the blue like a car accident happening very close to home on a street with low traffic volume while the person was driving slowly. Who could have expected that their tire suddenly blew out, causing the car to suddenly plunge towards a tree and a large branch just happens to break the windshield and impale the driver? People can understand that many driving scenarios lend itself to some amount of danger, but certain scenarios just defy logic and expectation.<br /> <br /> Or what about the solider who survives a long war and finally on the way back home, only to be killed in an accident just before he makes it back home to his family safe? Or the young person who somehow develops cancer that normally only strikes people in their old age? None of these deaths are &quot;supposed&quot; to happen. Tragic or even ironic deaths can happen to anyone. But senseless deaths leave people confused and perplexed because they seem to imply a lack of order in the universe that people don't like.<br /> <br /> It's this lack of meaning or understanding of their deaths, why they died, is what puts them collectively into the Penitent Legion. I think anytime someone could ask, &quot;Why or how did this happen to me?&quot; is a candidate for the legion. The original purposes of the legions were to help people reconcile with their deaths and be able to Transcend. And all of the people who would qualify for the Penitent Legion who would have the same kind of questions - &quot;how could this have possibly happened to me?&quot; So common experience in helping one wraith accept what happened to them so they could move at least theoretically should be able to help others. &quot;Madness&quot; need not be personal, but in the sense there is no purpose in the world.<br /> <br /> That also explains why the truly mad are also placed here because mental illness is also seen as inexplicable so that they still fit. For me, the stereotypical &quot;victim of madness&quot; still fits the Legion, but doesn't really define it.<br /> <br /> The Penitent Legion is a good way for the ST to address these kind of issues. The other legions not so much. The Penitent Legion, Legion of Paupers, and Legion of Fate are small legions where the ST (and some players) can have a bit of fun or creativity to explore certain aspects of mortality that don't really come up elsewhere. They aren't meant to be equals to the other legions, and that's fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}